            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioner, the Chickasaw and Choctaw Nations asked the Court to hold that Congress has afforded tribes the same exemption from Federal wagering taxes enjoyed by State governmental wagering operations."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Overview",
    "status": "complete",
    "summary": null,
    "summary_citations": []
  },
  "party_positions": [
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioner",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "a3901e090d59690756e35310107c7847d7e153b8ab504e01526c100a8098da2e",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-a3901e090d59-0000-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 3,
                "start": 3
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioner, the Chickasaw Nation argues that Congress has given tribes the same exemption from federal wagering taxes as states."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Luthey",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "a3901e090d59690756e35310107c7847d7e153b8ab504e01526c100a8098da2e",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-a3901e090d59-0001-0002"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 20,
                "start": 20
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Luthey, the Tribe relies on the precedent that statutes concerning Indians that are ambiguous are to be construed in favor of the Indians (Montana v. Blackfeet Tribe)."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Luthey",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "a3901e090d59690756e35310107c7847d7e153b8ab504e01526c100a8098da2e",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-a3901e090d59-0002-0003"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 46,
                "start": 46
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Luthey, the Tribe points to IGRA \u00a72719(d)(2), which states that the provisions of the subsection (including the statute at issue) shall apply notwithstanding any other provision of law, supporting the exemption claim."
        }
      ],
      "heading": "Chickasaw Nation (Petitioner)",
      "status": "complete",
      "summary": null,
      "summary_citations": []
    },
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. DuMont",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "a3901e090d59690756e35310107c7847d7e153b8ab504e01526c100a8098da2e",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-a3901e090d59-0002-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 41,
                "start": 40
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. DuMont, the United States argues that the statute is not ambiguous and therefore does not provide an exemption for tribal wagering."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. DuMont",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "a3901e090d59690756e35310107c7847d7e153b8ab504e01526c100a8098da2e",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-a3901e090d59-0002-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 38,
                "start": 38
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. DuMont, the United States contends that the canon of construction is not a true tie\u2011breaker in this case and should not be used to infer an exemption."
        }
      ],
      "heading": "United States (Respondent)",
      "status": "complete",
